Let the equations of the planes be
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} x + 2y + 3z &= 5 \\ x - y + 2z &= 7 \\ 3x - 3y + kz &= 10\end{align*}
For the planes to not have a unique point of intersection, the determinant of the coefficients must be zero. The coefficient matrix is
\[ \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\ 3 & -3 & k \end{pmatrix} \]
The determinant of this matrix is
\begin{align*} 1((-1)(k) - (2)(-3)) - 2((1)(k) - (2)(3)) + 3((1)(-3) - (-1)(3)) &= 0 \\ 1(-k + 6) - 2(k - 6) + 3(-3 + 3) &= 0 \\ -k + 6 - 2k + 12 + 0 &= 0 \\ -3k + 18 &= 0 \\ 3k &= 18 \\ k &= 6 \end{align*}
Now we need to check if the system is consistent when $k=6$. The equations become
\begin{align*} x + 2y + 3z &= 5 \\ x - y + 2z &= 7 \\ 3x - 3y + 6z &= 10 \end{align*}
We can write the third equation as $3(x - y + 2z) = 10$. Since $x - y + 2z = 7$, we have $3(7) = 10$, which is $21 = 10$, a contradiction. Thus, the system is inconsistent when $k=6$.

Final answer: 6

